---
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";
---

<Doc
	title="Privacy policy"
	description="Paperwren's privacy policy. The app collects nothing and has no analytics or ads. The Android app never connects to the internet; on a computer, only an update check you allow does. Here is what stays on your device and how to clear it."
	label="Privacy policy"
	heading="Paperwren collects <em>nothing.</em>"
	path="/privacy/"
	updated="27 October 2026"
>
	<p class="lead">
		Paperwren is a document viewer with a few PDF tools. This policy is short because Paperwren
		collects almost nothing, and what it keeps never leaves your device.
	</p>

	<h2>What Paperwren collects</h2>
	<p>
		Nothing. Paperwren does not collect, transmit, sell or share any personal
		data. It has no analytics, no advertising, no trackers and no crash
		reporting. The Android app does not connect to the internet at all; on
		Windows and macOS the only connection is the update check below, and only
		if you allow it.
	</p>

	<h2>Update checks on Windows and macOS</h2>
	<p>
		The first time it runs on a computer, Paperwren asks whether to check for
		updates. If you agree, each time it starts it downloads a small file
		listing the latest version from GitHub, where Paperwren is published. If
		there is a newer version, it asks before downloading and installing it.
		The request carries nothing about you or your files; like any download,
		GitHub sees your IP address and may keep standard server logs. You can
		turn this off, or check by hand, in Settings under Updates. The Android
		app never checks: it updates through the store or the APK you install.
	</p>

	<h2>What stays on your device</h2>
	<h3>Recent files</h3>
	<p>
		The names and locations of documents you open, their size and where you
		stopped reading, so the app can list them and resume. You can turn this
		off, limit it, or clear it in Settings.
	</p>
	<h3>Private copies of files</h3>
	<p>
		On Android, when you open a file from the picker or from another app,
		Paperwren keeps a private copy that only it can read, so the file still
		opens from your recents after you close the app. Each file is stored once,
		copies are capped at 250 MB in total (oldest removed first), and you can
		delete them all in Settings.
	</p>
	<h3>Your settings</h3>
	<p>Choices like your theme and how PDFs open.</p>
	<h3>PDF passwords</h3>
	<p>
		Used in memory to open that file, then forgotten. They are never written
		anywhere. A password you give a copy with "Add a password" is used to lock
		that copy and is not kept.
	</p>
	<h3>Signatures you keep</h3>
	<p>
		When you sign a PDF, you can ask Paperwren to keep your signature or
		initials for next time. They are stored as pictures in the app's private
		storage on your device, and you can forget each one when you next sign.
	</p>
	<h3>Your notes</h3>
	<p>
		Notes you write in Paperwren are stored in the app's private storage on
		your device. They go nowhere unless you save one as a PDF or back them up,
		which writes a file where you choose. Removing the app removes them.
	</p>
	<h3>Copies you save</h3>
	<p>
		Files the tools make (a signed form, merged pages) are saved only where
		you choose in your system's save screen.
	</p>

	<h2>Permissions</h2>
	<p>
		The Android app requests no permissions at all, including internet access.
		It only sees the files you open with it or share to it. Android's cloud
		backup is turned off for Paperwren, so its data does not travel with your
		backups either.
	</p>

	<h2>Files you open</h2>
	<p>
		Documents are read and displayed on your device only. Paperwren never
		uploads them or sends anything about them anywhere.
	</p>

	<h2>Deleting your data</h2>
	<p>
		Clear recents and private copies in Settings, or uninstall the app, which
		removes everything it stored. There is no account, so there is nothing to
		delete anywhere else.
	</p>

	<h2>Children</h2>
	<p>Paperwren collects no data from anyone, including children.</p>

	<h2>This website</h2>
	<p>
		This site sets no cookies, runs no analytics and serves its own fonts and
		scripts. Your browser talks only to the host serving these pages, which may
		keep standard server logs.
	</p>

	<h2>Changes</h2>
	<p>
		If a future version ever changes any of this, this page will be updated and
		the app will tell you before the change takes effect.
	</p>

	<h2>Contact</h2>
	<p>
		Questions about this policy or the app: email
		<a class="link" href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>, or open an
		issue on the project's <a class="link" href={SITE.issues} rel="noopener">issue tracker</a>.
	</p>
</Doc>

